===Verb===
{{en-verb|grows|growing|grew|grown|past2=growed|past2_qual=dialectal|past_ptc2=growed|past_ptc2_qual=dialectal}}

# {{lb|en|ergative}} To [[become]] [[larger]], to [[increase]] in [[magnitude]].
#: {{ux|en|Children '''grow''' quickly.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=December|title=Talking of trains: B.R. safety in 1959|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=708|text=[...] but the dangers to trespassers, especially children, are '''growing''', and a vigorous educational programme is urged.}}
# {{lb|en|ergative|of plants}} To [[undergo]] [[growth]]; to [[be]] [[present]] {{gloss|somewhere}}
#: {{ux|en|Apples now '''grow''' all over the world.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[appear]] or [[sprout]].
#: {{ux|en|Leaf buds '''grew''' on the trees with the advance of spring.}}
#: {{ux|en|A long tail began to '''grow''' from his backside.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[develop]], to [[mature]].
#: {{ux|en|As I '''grew''' throughout adolescence, I came to appreciate many things about human nature.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause or allow something to become bigger, especially to [[cultivate]] plants.
#: {{ux|en|He '''grows''' peppers and squash each summer in his garden.}}
#: {{ux|en|Have you ever '''grown''' your hair before?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=March 01, 2011|author=Peter Roff| title=Another Foolish Move By Congress| work=Fox News| url=http://www.foxnews.com/opinion/2011/03/01/foolish-congress/| passage=''The Bush administration – which sought to '''grow''' the number of fisheries managed under a program known as “catch shares”...''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-07-10|author=w:James Poniewozik|title=The Twitter Watch Party Is Over|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/10/arts/television/tv-twitter-end-of-an-era.html|passage=And — again to overgeneralize from my experience — users may not want a second Twitter either. I was a heavy Twitter user for over a decade. I loved it until I didn’t. I made connections, '''grew''' a following, floated ideas, had fun. But it also became a second, often angry, voice inside my head. Do I want to replace it with another one?}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# {{lb|en|copulative}} To [[assume]] a condition or quality over time.
#: {{ux|en|The boy '''grew''' wise as he matured.}}
#: {{ux|en|The town '''grew''' smaller and smaller in the distance as we travelled.}}
#: {{ux|en|You have '''grown''' strong.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1967|author=w:Barbara Sleigh| title=w:Jessamy| year_published=1993| location=Sevenoaks, Kent| publisher=Bloomsbury| isbn=0 340 19547 9| page=18|passage=In fact she was so bus doing all the things that anyone might, who finds themselves alone in an empty house, that she did not notice at first when it began to turn dusk and the rooms '''to grow''' dim.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To become attached or fixed; to adhere.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|V|iii|passage=Our knees shall kneel till to the ground they '''grow'''.}}
#* {{seemoreCites|en}}<!-- Please no more than 3–4 quotes here, further quotes only in 'Citations' (see [[WT:QUOTE]])-->

